Why this stack (and not Flutter)
You said React. This is the right call. TypeScript + React Native via Expo is the most Copilot-friendly mobile stack that exists — Copilot was trained on vastly more React code than Dart/Flutter, so autocomplete quality is dramatically better. You write one language (TypeScript) across mobile, desktop, and any future web surface. The Expo toolchain is the easiest mobile dev experience available: you scan a QR code and your phone runs the app live. No cables, no emulator setup required to get started.

 
SECTION 1 — The Stack, Explained

1.1 TypeScript — The Language
TypeScript is JavaScript with types. If you've never written either, think of it as a language that looks like this:

const daysUntil = (targetDate: Date): number => {
  const now = new Date();
  const diff = targetDate.getTime() - now.getTime();
  return Math.floor(diff / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
};

•	Types catch mistakes before you run anything. If you try to pass a string where a number is expected, your editor underlines it in red immediately.
•	Copilot is exceptionally good at TypeScript. Because types tell Copilot exactly what shape data is, its suggestions are far more accurate than in plain JavaScript.
•	Every file ends in .ts (logic) or .tsx (logic that also renders UI components). You will mostly write .tsx files.

1.2 Expo — Your Mobile Development Platform
Expo is a toolkit built on top of React Native that handles the painful parts of mobile development for you. React Native lets you write JavaScript that renders as native Android and iOS UI. Expo wraps React Native and adds:

•	Expo Go — a free app you install on your Android phone. You open it, scan a QR code from your terminal, and your app is running on your phone. No USB cable. No Android Studio setup for testing. Live reload every time you save a file.
•	The Expo SDK — a library of pre-built native modules: camera, file system, SQLite, notifications, sensors, haptics. You import them like any npm package. No native code required.
•	EAS Build — Expo's cloud build service. When you want to produce a real .apk file to install on your phone (not just via Expo Go), EAS builds it in the cloud. Free tier available.

Expo Go vs standalone APK
While developing, you use Expo Go on your phone — it's instant and requires no build step. When you want to test the full app with all features (background tasks, notifications, system tray), you build a standalone APK. You do this once per major milestone, not constantly. For the first two weeks, Expo Go is all you need.

1.3 Electron — Your Desktop App
Electron packages a web app (HTML + CSS + JavaScript) into a standalone desktop application. The same app that runs in a browser runs in a window on your computer with access to the file system, system tray, and OS notifications. Discord, VS Code, Slack, and Notion are all Electron apps.

Here is the key insight for FlowState: you will build the desktop app as a React web app first (it runs in your browser for development), and then Electron wraps it to make it a real desktop app. The desktop app shares almost all its business logic with the mobile app via a shared package. You maintain one set of database functions, one Firebase sync layer, one set of module types — and the UI components are the only thing that differs between mobile (React Native) and desktop (React web).

The monorepo structure
The cleanest way to share code between Expo and Electron is a monorepo: one repository with multiple packages. You'll have: packages/core (shared TypeScript logic — timer engine, CSV parser, module specs, Firebase sync), apps/mobile (Expo React Native app), and apps/desktop (React + Electron app). Both apps import from packages/core. This is the architecture used by every serious cross-platform product.

1.4 Firebase — Cloud Sync (same as v2-v4 spec)
•	Firebase Auth: anonymous sign-in on first launch, optional Google account linking later
•	Firebase Firestore: real-time cloud database. When you pause a timer on your phone, the desktop app receives the update in under a second.
•	Firestore offline persistence: built into the Firebase SDK. Your data is cached locally. The app works with no internet.
•	Free Spark plan: 50,000 reads and 20,000 writes per day. More than enough for personal use.

1.5 SQLite — Local Database
•	expo-sqlite: SQLite database on the phone. A file stored on device. The source of truth for all your data when offline.
•	better-sqlite3: the same SQLite but for the Electron desktop app. Identical SQL queries, different package.
•	Drizzle ORM: a TypeScript ORM that works with both expo-sqlite and better-sqlite3. You write your schema once in TypeScript, Drizzle generates the SQL, and you get typed query results. This is the equivalent of Drift from the Flutter guide.

1.6 Zustand — State Management
Zustand is a tiny state management library for React. Where Redux is a complex ceremony, Zustand is just a store object with a set function. The timer state, the current day plan, the active module values — all of these live in Zustand stores that any component can read or update.

// Example Zustand store
const useTimerStore = create<TimerState>((set) => ({
  phase: 'idle',
  startedAt: null,
  blockIndex: 0,
  play: (routineId) => set({ phase: 'running', startedAt: Date.now(), routineId }),
  pause: () => set((s) => ({ phase: 'paused', pausedAt: Date.now() })),
}));

Copilot is excellent at generating Zustand stores. Once you write the first one, it will autocomplete the rest of your stores almost perfectly.

