SECTION 2 — Setting Up Your Machine

Do every step in order. The whole setup takes about 45 minutes the first time.

2.1 Install Node.js
Node.js is the runtime that everything in this stack runs on. npm (Node Package Manager) comes bundled with it and is how you install all packages.

1	Go to nodejs.org
Download the LTS version (the left button, labelled 'LTS'). Install it. Accept all defaults.

2	Verify the install
Open a terminal (Command Prompt on Windows, Terminal on macOS) and run: node --version. You should see something like v20.x.x. Then run: npm --version. You should see 10.x.x or similar.

2.2 Install VS Code + Extensions
1	Download VS Code
Go to code.visualstudio.com. Download and install. Open it.

2	Install GitHub Copilot
Click the Extensions icon in the left sidebar (four squares icon). Search 'GitHub Copilot'. Install it. Then search 'GitHub Copilot Chat' and install that too. Sign in with your GitHub account when prompted. Copilot requires a GitHub account — students get it free via GitHub Education.

3	Install these extensions
Search and install each: ESLint (code quality), Prettier (auto-formatting), TypeScript Importer (auto-imports), React Native Tools (debugging), Expo Tools (Expo integration).

4	Configure auto-save
Go to File > Preferences > Settings. Search 'auto save'. Set it to 'onFocusChange'. This means files save automatically when you click away, which triggers Expo's live reload instantly.

2.3 Install the Expo CLI
1	Run this in your terminal
npm install -g expo-cli  The -g flag installs it globally, meaning you can run 'expo' from any folder.

2	Install Expo Go on your phone
Open the Google Play Store on your Android phone. Search 'Expo Go'. Install it. Sign in with or create an Expo account (free).

2.4 Create Your Firebase Project
1	Go to Firebase Console
console.firebase.google.com. Sign in with Google. Click 'Add Project'. Name it 'flowstate'. Disable Google Analytics (not needed). Create.

2	Set up Firestore
In the left sidebar: Firestore Database > Create Database > Start in Test Mode > select a region close to you > Enable.

3	Set up Authentication
In the left sidebar: Authentication > Get Started > Sign-in method tab > Anonymous > Enable > Save.

4	Get your config keys
In Project Settings (gear icon) > Your apps > Add app > Web app (</> icon). Register the app with any nickname. Copy the firebaseConfig object that appears — you'll paste this into your project later.

2.5 Install Git (if not already installed)
1	Check if Git is installed
In your terminal: git --version. If you see a version number, skip this step.

2	If not installed
Windows: download from git-scm.com. macOS: run 'xcode-select --install' in terminal. Install with all defaults.

3	Create a GitHub account
Go to github.com if you don't have one. You need this for Copilot and for version control.

 
SECTION 3 — Creating the Project

This section creates the actual FlowState project on your computer. Run every command exactly as written.

3.1 Create the Monorepo
Open your terminal. Navigate to where you keep your projects (e.g. cd ~/Documents or cd C:\Projects). Then run:

npx create-turbo@latest flowstate

Turbo is a monorepo tool from Vercel. When prompted:
•	Which package manager: npm
•	It will create a flowstate/ folder with a packages/ and apps/ directory

cd flowstate
code .

VS Code opens with the whole monorepo. You'll see the file tree on the left.

3.2 Clean Up the Template
Turbo creates some example apps you don't need. Delete everything in apps/ and packages/ except the turbo.json and package.json at the root. Your folder should look like:

flowstate/
  apps/          (empty)
  packages/      (empty)
  package.json
  turbo.json

3.3 Create the Mobile App (Expo)
cd apps
npx create-expo-app mobile --template blank-typescript
cd ..

This creates apps/mobile/ with a fresh Expo app in TypeScript. To test it immediately:
cd apps/mobile
npx expo start

A QR code appears in your terminal. Open Expo Go on your phone and scan it. You should see a white screen with 'Open up App.tsx to start working on your app!'. That's your app running live on your phone. Every time you save a file in VS Code, the phone updates instantly.

Keep this terminal running
The 'npx expo start' process is your development server. Keep it running in one terminal tab while you code. Open a second terminal tab for running other commands.

3.4 Create the Desktop App (Electron + React)
cd apps
npm create vite@latest desktop -- --template react-ts
cd desktop
npm install

This creates apps/desktop/ with a React + TypeScript + Vite app. Vite is a fast development server for web apps. Then add Electron:

npm install electron electron-builder --save-dev
npm install concurrently --save-dev

Create a file at apps/desktop/electron/main.js with this content (Copilot will fill this in — just type the comment and let it autocomplete):

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Create an Electron main process file that opens a browser window loading the Vite dev server on localhost:5173 in development, and the built index.html in production. Add system tray support with a context menu.”

3.5 Create the Shared Core Package
cd packages
mkdir core && cd core
npm init -y

Edit the generated package.json to add: "name": "@flowstate/core", "main": "src/index.ts". Then create the folder structure:

packages/core/src/
  index.ts          ← exports everything
  timer/            ← timer engine
  modules/          ← module spec types
  csv/              ← CSV parser
  firebase/         ← sync logic
  db/               ← Drizzle schema (shared between mobile + desktop)
  types/            ← shared TypeScript interfaces

3.6 Connect the Packages
In apps/mobile/package.json and apps/desktop/package.json, add to dependencies:
"@flowstate/core": "*"

Then from the root:
npm install

Turbo links the packages together. You can now import from '@flowstate/core' in both apps:
import { TimerEngine } from '@flowstate/core';

3.7 Set Up Firebase in the Core Package
In packages/core/src/firebase/, create a config.ts file:

// packages/core/src/firebase/config.ts
import { initializeApp } from 'firebase/app';
import { getFirestore } from 'firebase/firestore';
import { getAuth } from 'firebase/auth';

const firebaseConfig = {
  apiKey: 'YOUR_API_KEY',          // paste from Firebase console
  authDomain: 'YOUR_AUTH_DOMAIN',
  projectId: 'YOUR_PROJECT_ID',
  // ... rest of config
};

export const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
export const db = getFirestore(app);
export const auth = getAuth(app);

Never commit your Firebase keys to GitHub
Create a .env file at the root of each app and put keys there. Add .env to your .gitignore file. Reference them as process.env.FIREBASE_API_KEY in code. Copilot will remind you of this if you ask: 'How should I handle environment variables securely in this project?'

 
