SECTION 5 — The Build: Phase by Phase

PHASE 1
UI Shell — Fake Data, Real Screens	Days 1–3

Goal: Every screen built with hardcoded fake data. No database. No network. No CSV. At the end of Phase 1, you can navigate the full app on your phone, see the Homescreen, tap into a day, see modules, and tap into a session card. Copilot will write 60% of this phase for you if you learn to prompt it correctly.

React Native Concepts for Phase 1
•	In React Native, there are no HTML elements. Instead of <div> you use <View>. Instead of <p> you use <Text>. Instead of <img> you use <Image>. The mental model is identical to React, just different primitives.
•	StyleSheet.create() defines styles in JavaScript objects instead of CSS files. { flex: 1 } is like 'height: 100%'. { flexDirection: 'row' } is like 'display: flex; flex-direction: row'.
•	expo-router uses file-based routing: if you create app/week/[weekId].tsx, navigating to /week/2026-W08 opens that file. No router config required.
•	Copilot tip: after typing a component name like <TimerRing, press Tab. Copilot will write the entire component. After a comment like // Step counter module with progress bar, press Enter. Copilot will write the whole component.

Phase 1 Tasks

1.1	Set up expo-router navigation
Install expo-router: npx expo install expo-router. Create the app/ folder structure from the project layout above. Create placeholder screen files that just return a <View><Text>Screen Name</Text></View>.

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Create an expo-router tab layout with three tabs: Home (house icon), Today (calendar icon), and Plan (list icon). Use a dark background with purple accent colour #6C63FF.”

1.2	Build the Homescreen
Three zones stacked in a ScrollView: LiveZone (a 2x2 grid of placeholder cards), TodaySnapshot (a card with title, must-do count, session chips, and a large button), LoggedModulesZone (a column of module cards).

1.3	Build the Day Dashboard screen
A ScrollView with: a header showing date and day title, a checklist of 3 hardcoded must-do items (each with a Pressable checkbox), a row of module cards, and 2 session cards with Play buttons.

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Create a React Native CheckboxModule component that shows a label, an optional emoji, a checkbox that toggles on press with a haptic tick, and a streak counter beneath it. Use TypeScript with proper props interface.”

1.4	Build the three module card types
Create components/modules/: CheckboxCard.tsx, RatingCard.tsx (5 star tap interaction), DataInputCard.tsx (numeric TextInput with unit label and progress bar). Hardcode values for now, onValueChange does nothing yet.

1.5	Build the Routine Player skeleton
A full-screen View with: a circular TimerRing component in the centre (CircularProgress from react-native-circular-progress or built with SVG), the block name below it, a row of three buttons: Pause, Skip, End. Hardcode '25:00' as the display.

1.6	Build the countdown module card
A card showing a large number (the days count), the label, and a subtitle. Hardcode 136 days. No live computation yet.

End of Phase 1
You can navigate the full app on your phone. Every screen exists. Module cards show and react to taps. Nothing saves, nothing is real — but the whole structure is there and looks right.

PHASE 2
Local Storage & The Timer Engine	Days 4–7

Goal: The timer counts down correctly, survives the app being backgrounded, and shows a persistent notification. Module values save and reload. Routines persist between app launches.

Key Concepts for Phase 2
•	Zustand stores hold your in-memory app state. The timer store holds phase, startedAt, blockIndex. A useEffect with setInterval reads from the store every second and triggers a re-render of the timer display.
•	Drizzle schema: you define your tables in packages/core/src/db/schema.ts using Drizzle's schema builder. Run 'npx drizzle-kit push' to apply them to the SQLite file.
•	expo-sqlite: use SQLiteProvider at the root of your app to provide a database connection. Use useSQLiteContext() in any component to get it. Drizzle wraps this connection.
•	Always-visible timer notification: use expo-notifications to show a notification with the running timer text. Update it every second from a background task using expo-task-manager.

Phase 2 Tasks

2.1	Set up Drizzle + SQLite
Install: npx expo install expo-sqlite drizzle-orm. Install drizzle-kit as dev dependency. Define your schema in packages/core/src/db/schema.ts: routines, routineBlocks, dayPlans, moduleSpecs, moduleValues, sessions, eventLog tables.

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Write a Drizzle ORM schema in TypeScript for a FlowState app with tables: routines (id, name, color, createdAt), routineBlocks (id, routineId, name, durationSeconds, order), dayPlans (date primary key, title, mustDoJson, status), moduleSpecs (id, type, label, emoji, configJson, placementsJson, isLive), moduleValues (id, moduleSpecId, contextKey, valueJson, loggedAt).”

2.2	Build the TimerEngine in shared core
In packages/core/src/timer/TimerEngine.ts: a class that stores startedAt, pausedAt, totalPausedMs, blockDuration, blockIndex. The remaining getter computes time from timestamps, never a running counter. Methods: play(), pause(), resume(), skip(), end().

2.3	Build the timer Zustand store
In apps/mobile/hooks/useTimerStore.ts: a Zustand store that holds TimerState and delegates to TimerEngine. Connect the RoutinePlayer screen to this store. Add a setInterval in a useEffect that ticks every second and forces a re-render of the TimerRing.

2.4	Wire module values to SQLite
When a module card value changes (checkbox tapped, number entered, rating selected), call an insertModuleValue() or updateModuleValue() function from packages/core/src/db/queries.ts. When a screen loads, query moduleValues for today's date and initialise cards with stored values.

2.5	Background timer notification
Use expo-notifications to register and show a notification. Use expo-task-manager to define a TIMER_TICK background task. Every second while a session is running, update the notification text to show the remaining time and block name. Edit app.json to add the required notification permissions.

End of Phase 2
The timer ticks correctly on your phone. Background it, come back, it's still right. Enter values into modules — restart the app, values are still there. The app works completely offline with zero network activity.

PHASE 3
CSV Import — The Core Feature	Days 8–10

Goal: Pick a .csv file from your phone's storage. See a preview of what it will import. Tap import. The Week view fills with day titles, session cards, module targets. This is the feature that makes FlowState useful.

Key Concepts for Phase 3
•	expo-document-picker: FilePickerResult = await DocumentPicker.getDocumentAsync({ type: 'text/comma-separated-values' }). Returns the file URI. Use expo-file-system to read its contents: const content = await FileSystem.readAsStringAsync(uri).
•	csv-parse: a Node.js compatible CSV parser that works in React Native. parse(content, { columns: true, skip_empty_lines: true }) gives you an array of objects where keys are the column headers.
•	Zod validation: define a Zod schema that matches your CSV row shape. Parse each row through it. Zod returns detailed error messages on failure — perfect for the validation preview screen.
•	Plan-First upsert: use Drizzle's .onConflictDoUpdate() to upsert day plans — insert if new, update plan fields if existing. In the update set, exclude tracking columns (done, any logged module values).

Phase 3 Tasks

3.1	Build the CSV parser in shared core
In packages/core/src/csv/parser.ts: a function that takes raw CSV string and returns ParsedPlan { days: ParsedDay[], errors: string[] }. ParsedDay has date, title, mustDo (split on ;), sessions array, moduleTargets map.

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Write a TypeScript CSV parser for FlowState. It should parse columns: date (YYYY-MM-DD), title, must_do (semicolon-separated), session_1_routine through session_5_routine with optional _time and _label, target_ prefixed columns for module targets, require_ prefixed columns for required flags. Return a typed ParsedPlan object. Use the csv-parse library.”

3.2	Build the validator
In packages/core/src/csv/validator.ts: a function that takes ParsedPlan and a list of known routine IDs from the database, and returns ValidationResult { errors: ValidationError[], warnings: string[] }. Errors block import. Warnings show but allow import.

3.3	Build the import UI flow
Three screens in expo-router: app/import/pick.tsx (document picker button + selected file name), app/import/preview.tsx (shows row count, date range, routines found/missing, modules detected, errors and warnings list, Import and Cancel buttons), app/import/success.tsx.

3.4	Build the import writer
In packages/core/src/db/queries.ts: importPlan(days: ParsedDay[]) function. For each day: upsert the dayPlans row (plan fields only), upsert moduleValue rows for target columns, create pending session records. Never touch existing logged values.

3.5	Test with a real CSV
Generate a 14-row test CSV. Import it. Verify Week view shows titles, Day view shows must-do items and session cards, module cards show correct targets.

End of Phase 3
FlowState does its core job: you plan outside (spreadsheet, AI, Notion export), you import the plan, FlowState executes it day by day. The 136-day holiday plan is importable in 30 seconds.

PHASE 4
Countdown Modules & the Homescreen	Days 11–12

Goal: The Homescreen is live. Countdown and countup modules compute and display in real time. The module manager lets you add, configure, and pin modules to the Homescreen.

4.1	Build the CountdownCard component
Reads config.targetDate and config.startDate from the ModuleSpec. Uses a useMemo to compute remaining days/hours/minutes/seconds from Date.now(). Uses a useEffect with setInterval(fn, 1000) to update the display. Shows a progress bar if showProgressBar is true in config.

GitHub Copilot prompt:
“Write a React Native CountdownCard component in TypeScript. Props: moduleSpec (ModuleSpec type), compact (boolean for homescreen vs detail view). It should compute the remaining time from the targetDate in config, update every second, show days/hours/minutes/seconds, and optionally show a LinearGradient progress bar showing elapsed vs remaining time.”

4.2	Build the CountupCard component
Same pattern but computing elapsed time since config.originDate. Supports displayMode: 'days', 'dhms', 'years_days'. Shows milestone badges when elapsed days hit a milestone value from config.

4.3	Build the Module Manager screen
A FlatList of all ModuleSpecs in the database. Each row shows the emoji, label, type badge, and a toggle for whether it's pinned to the Homescreen. A + button opens a bottom sheet: pick type → fill config fields → choose placements → save.

4.4	Wire the Homescreen zones to real data
Replace hardcoded cards with data from Zustand + SQLite. Zone 1: query moduleSpecs where isLive = true and homescreen is in placements, ordered by homescreenOrder. Zone 3: query moduleSpecs where isLive = false and homescreen is in placements.

End of Phase 4
The Homescreen is real. Your holiday countdown ticks down. Your birthday countup counts up. You can add new modules, configure them, and pin them to the Homescreen. The app is genuinely useful.

PHASE 5
Cloud Sync & the Desktop App	Days 13–16

Goal: Pause the timer on your phone, watch it pause on your laptop. Import a CSV on the desktop app, it appears on your phone. The desktop app has a system tray icon with the live timer.

5.1	Add Firebase Auth to shared core
In packages/core/src/firebase/auth.ts: signInAnonymously() on first launch, store the UID in Zustand and in AsyncStorage (mobile) / localStorage (desktop). All Firestore paths are /users/{uid}/...

5.2	Build the SyncService
In packages/core/src/firebase/sync.ts: two functions. pushToFirestore(collection, docId, data): tries the write, on failure adds to a local CommandQueue table. listenForRemoteChanges(collection, docIds, onUpdate): subscribes to Firestore snapshots, filters out changes from the local device using a deviceId field, calls onUpdate with the new data.

5.3	Sync timer state across devices
The timerStore calls SyncService.pushToFirestore('timerState', 'active', timerState) on every state change. A useEffect on mount calls listenForRemoteChanges for the timerState document. When a remote change arrives, update the local Zustand store.

5.4	Build the desktop app UI
